//? Title: False Position and Illinois: True Brackets, Weighted Secants
//? Subtitle: Actual callback histories, matched stopping rules and finite-arithmetic boundaries
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Locate a zero of a continuous scalar function from a sign-changing bracket.
False position replaces the midpoint with a secant intercept; Illinois changes
interpolation weights when an endpoint remains stale. Starting with x²−2 on
[1,2], actual native records distinguish true endpoint values from those
artificial weights. We retain the original exponential example, compare both
methods at the same tolerance, and examine stalls, rescaled functions and
full-width finite inputs. One checked C++ kernel serves ordinary results,
work summaries and owned same-pass callback/step traces. Numerical stopping
conditions are stated separately from mathematical root guarantees.
)

\toc

> Three hand-checkable trials and the first weight change

For f(x)=x²−2, the endpoints one and two have values −1 and two.
Their secant crosses zero at c=4/3, with actual callback value −2/9.
This replaces the negative endpoint. The next trial is 1.4 with value
−0.04, replacing that endpoint again. The native retained-twice Illinois
rule then halves the other endpoint's interpolation weight from two to one.
Its actual callback value remains f(2)=2.

The third secant uses a=1.4, weight −0.04 and b=2, weight one, producing
c≈1.4230769. The actual f(c) is positive, so the true bracket can change
sides. Artificial weights choose the secant; actual callback signs decide
which bracket endpoint to replace. The three-trial budget below deliberately
stops before convergence. All displayed values and branch flags are taken
from the native solve, without a second explanatory iteration loop.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/false_position_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
