\caption(The first and third actual trials use different secant weights. Orange identifies the retained endpoint whose true function value stays two while its artificial weight becomes one. The three-trial run ends at its budget, not a claimed root.)
\alttext(Two native secant-flow diagrams show actual endpoint coordinates, true function values, weights and trial values for the first and third evaluations of x squared minus two. The third-trial endpoint at two has true value two and orange weight one.)

> Derive the weighted trial and preserve the true sign bracket

Let w_a and w_b be nonzero, oppositely signed interpolation weights.
The line (1−t)w_a+t w_b crosses zero at

\[
t=\frac{|w_a|}{|w_a|+|w_b|},\qquad
c=(1-t)a+t b=\frac{w_ba-w_ab}{w_b-w_a}.
\]

In exact arithmetic 0<t<1, so the trial lies inside the endpoint interval,
regardless of their supplied order. Evaluate the actual f(c). If it is
nonzero, replace the endpoint whose true value has the same sign as f(c).
The remaining true values still have opposite signs. Under continuity and
reliable signs, the intermediate value theorem therefore keeps a root
inside the bracket. Never substitute a halved weight for a callback value
when checking this invariant or reporting a residual.

Plain regula falsi keeps both weights equal to the actual endpoint values.
For the native Illinois variant, two consecutive replacements of the same
endpoint trigger a halving of the opposite retained weight. No previous
replacement is assumed initially. This preserves the existing compiled API.
Dowell and Jarratt's most-recent-point initialization can damp after its
first trial; our starting rule differs, so its literal early iterates need
not reproduce their table. The stale-weight mechanism is the same.

Each ordinary iteration adds one callback evaluation after two initial
endpoint evaluations. Ordinary calls use constant auxiliary storage. Traces
retain those actual calls and pre-update steps in the same pass, with no
callback or input reference retained. Callback exceptions propagate, and
side effects may already have occurred when a later failure is raised.

> Retain the original exponential example and compare equal tolerances

The original call solves exp(x)−2 on [0,1] with the default residual tolerance
1e−10. It still returns after seven trials. The old report's local comparison
used 1e−13 instead, so its eight Illinois trials were a different stopping
request. Both histories now come from the core, with matched controls:
plain and Illinois use the same 1e−13 tolerance and forty-trial budget.

The plot floors absolute residuals at 1e−16 solely before taking log10 for
display. Actual final residuals and stop reasons remain printed without that
floor. A callback value of zero is a floating result, not a mathematical
certificate of an exactly represented logarithm.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/false_position_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <cfenv>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto f=[](double x){return std::exp(x)-2;};const auto legacy=dm::algorithms::false_position(f,0,1);std::print("Original default: root {:.12f}; ln(2) {:.12f}; trials {}; actual residual {:.9g}.\n",legacy.root,std::log(2.),legacy.iterations,legacy.residual);auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::print("\n| policy | residual tolerance | trials | callback calls | captured records | actual final residual | stop |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");for(auto policy:{dm::algorithms::FalsePositionPolicy::Plain,dm::algorithms::FalsePositionPolicy::Illinois}){dm::algorithms::FalsePositionOptions o{policy,1e-13,0,40};const auto t=dm::algorithms::false_position_trace(f,0,1,o);std::vector<double>x,y;for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.steps.size();++i){x.push_back(i+1);y.push_back(std::log10(std::max(std::abs(t.steps[i].f_estimate),1e-16)));}const auto name=std::string(dm::algorithms::false_position_policy_name(policy));const auto colour=policy==dm::algorithms::FalsePositionPolicy::Plain?dm::graphics::RGB{220,38,38}:dm::graphics::RGB{37,99,235};plot.line(x,y,name,colour,2).points(x,y,"",colour,4);std::print("| {} | 1e-13 | {} | {} | {} | {:.9g} | {} |\n",name,t.outcome.result.iterations,t.outcome.evaluations,t.captured_entries,t.outcome.result.residual,dm::algorithms::false_position_stop_name(t.outcome.stop));if(!t.outcome.result.converged)throw std::runtime_error("original matched run failed");}
  plot.title("Original exponential problem: matched residual tolerances").x_label("Actual trial index").y_label("log10 max(actual absolute residual, 1e-16)").size(1000,540).save_svg("build/false_position_original.svg");if(legacy.iterations!=7)throw std::runtime_error("original default changed");
}
