> A more pronounced stall and a finite trial budget

For x^p−1 on [0,2], a convex curve can keep the right endpoint fixed while
plain false position advances from the left. As p grows, the initial positive
value 2^p−1 becomes much larger than the negative value −1, placing trials
near the left endpoint. Illinois changes the retained positive weight after
repeated left replacements, allowing a different trial location.

The next program measures powers two through thirty-two with identical
residual tolerances and 2,000-trial budgets. Exhaustion is reported as such;
a finite-prefix plot cannot prove eventual convergence or its asymptotic
rate. The right panel displays the first eighty actual pre-update endpoint states for p=10,
so it shows bracket movement separately from callback residual reduction.
A residual stop may still leave a wide bracket.

Under smooth simple-root assumptions, the Illinois paper analyzes a repeating
three-evaluation pattern with cubic error improvement and an efficiency index
near the cube root of three. That is neither a universal per-trial rate nor
an unconditional guarantee for finite arithmetic, discontinuities or multiple
roots. We do not estimate a convergence order from these few plotted points.

