> Function units and coordinate tolerances are different controls

Multiplying f by a positive scale does not change its mathematical roots or
an exact-arithmetic unweighted secant. It does change a residual measured in
function units. Keeping a fixed 1e−10 residual tolerance for
10^−30(x²−2) accepts the first trial despite a substantial coordinate error.
Scaling that tolerance by the same factor restores a comparable request;
finite rounding can still alter an individual path.

The program also disables residual tolerance and requests a rounded maximum
trial-to-endpoint distance below 1e−10 in coordinate units. Under continuity
and reliable signs, a true root in that bracket has distance no greater than
its exact endpoint radius. The API's radius is rounded, not an independently
outward-rounded interval certificate. A positive coordinate tolerance does
not prove the existence of a root for a discontinuous function. Zero disables
either tolerance separately; callback zeros always stop.

The log plots use a 1e−16 display floor for zero or smaller measured errors
and radii. Printed values retain their actual values. A large-scale residual
may never become small enough before the interpolation rounds to an endpoint;
stagnation is reported explicitly instead of called convergence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/false_position_units.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
